[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver/tests/semver_solver_impl_test.py: The contracted postconditions and checked preconditions in low/semver_solver.pyi remain unexercised in several areas. Range parsing never exercises rejection of clauses that look well-formed but violate SemVer grammar: a trailing prerelease separator with no identifiers, prerelease identifiers that are empty or contain characters outside alphanumerics and hyphen, numeric prerelease identifiers with leading zeroes, versions with more than three dot-separated components, numeric components following a wildcard, and non-numeric non-wildcard components; the malformed-syntax precondition requires all of these to raise InvalidRangeError. Range parsing also never exercises comparators or hyphen bounds applied to full Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 versions carrying build metadata identifiers, even though the specification contracts full semantic versions with build tuples and requires build metadata to be ignored in precedence. Satisfaction testing never exercises the !=, <=, and > comparators, although the comparators-and-prefixes and boolean-match postconditions contract satisfaction for every listed comparator. Satisfaction testing never exercises comparator or prefix operators applied to wildcard expressions whose first component is itself a wildcard (bare wildcard plus the ==, !=, <, <=, >, ^, and ~ forms), although wildcard expressions are contracted for every listed operator and wildcard normalization into comparator inequalities is contracted. Caret expansion is exercised only for a non-zero leading component, so expansion at the first non-zero component boundary is unverified for the zero-major cases: wildcard major with zero major still present, zero major with non-zero minor, zero major and minor with a full patch, and zero major and minor with a wildcard patch. Finally, resolution testing never exercises the forward-checking invariant in which a selected candidate declares a dependency on an already-assigned package whose assigned version violates that requirement, although the unsatisfiable-conflict precondition contracts that mutually incompatible requirements raise ResolutionConflictError carrying the involved package names and a derivation explanation.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: The trailing fallback return at line 279 of the range operator expansion helper is structurally unreachable under low/semver_solver.pyi: the range clause token grammar admits only the relational operators, the equality and aliased equality forms, caret, and tilde, each of which is handled and returns by an earlier branch of the same expansion, and the helper is reached only from clause compilation with such an operator. No conforming range expression can reach that fallback, so prune this dead return or annotate it with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: operator is restricted by the range clause grammar)'.
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: Address feedback: the trailing fallback return in the range operator expansion helper is unreachable because the clause token grammar only admits relational, equality/aliased-equality, caret, and tilde operators, all of which return in earlier branches. Retained the fallback (required by the declared non-Optional return type for pyright) and annotated it exactly as requested with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: operator is restricted by the range clause grammar)'
[CHANGE] benchmark_1/parts/semver_solver/tests/semver_solver_impl_test.py: Address coverage feedback: add parse_range rejection of SemVer grammar violations (trailing prerelease separator, empty/invalid prerelease identifiers, numeric prerelease leading zeroes, >3 components, numeric-after-wildcard, non-numeric components); add build-metadata comparator and hyphen-bound parsing plus build-ignored satisfaction; add !=, <=, > comparator satisfaction; add wildcard-first expressions (*, ==*, !=*, <*, <=*, >*, ^*, ~*) parsing and normalization; add zero-major caret boundary expansion cases (^0.x, ^0.2.x, ^0.0.3, ^0.0.x); add forward-checking resolution conflict test where a candidate depends on an already-assigned incompatible package.
